[Music]  the work done by a cell is often not  visible to the human eye so to make it  more meaningful to us we can consider  the analogous work we might do as humans  so consider a bowl there lying at the  bottom of a hill we can imagine having  to exert a lot of energy to push that  Boulder to the top of the hill if we  then release the boulder at any point  before getting it all the way to the top  it will roll back downhill without  needing any help from us processes like  a boulder rolling down a hill that  happened without having to add any  additional effort or energy are called  spontaneous processes like pushing a  boulder up a hill that requires the  input of energy are called non  spontaneous many cellular processes are  spontaneous for example in the last  lesson we described how phospholipids  self-assemble into by layers when  dissolved in an aqueous solution such as  water or the cytosol we can now further  define this self-assembly as being a  spontaneous process on the other hand  when a cell has to perform a non  spontaneous process such as making a  protein it typically does so by  releasing the energy stored in a  molecule called ATP synthase used to  power so many cellular functions it is  vitally important for cell to be able to  produce large amounts of ATP with this  in mind let's examine one organelle in  particular the mitochondrion one of my  favorites as we mentioned briefly in the  last lesson the mitochondrion is the  functional module that allows cells to  maximize the amount of ATP that is  generated from the energy contained in  the food we and our cells consume  compartmentalization is crucial here  we're looking at a three dimensional  transmission e/m of a cell contrary to  the 2d representation that we usually  see the compartmentalization is clearer  and more evident the role of the  mitochondrion is quite specifically to  synthesize ATP each mitochondrion is  surrounded by a double membrane  structure made up of separate outer and  inner membranes the outer mitochondrial  membrane contains pouring channels  these are channels that allow ions and  most small molecules to diffuse into the  mitochondrion macromolecules such as  proteins and RNA molecules are way too  large to diffuse through the outer  membrane the outer membrane is also  extremely rich in enzymes associated  with mitochondrial lipid synthesis this  is critical for the division of  mitochondria indeed their reproduction  because to create more mitochondria you  need more membrane in contrast the inner  mitochondrial membrane is highly folded  into invaginations called cristae and it  surrounds the innermost compartment of  the mitochondrion called the matrix  cardiolipin is an abundant lipid in the  mitochondrial inner membrane however it  is not present in the mitochondrial  outer membrane which is primarily  composed of regular phospholipids  this helps to underscore just how  different those two membranes are unlike  the outer membrane the inner membrane is  highly impermeable to most small ions it  is composed of three major classes of  proteins that make up 76% of its mass  the first class of proteins are those  enzymes critical for catalyzing a subset  of reactions necessary for metabolism  the second class are transport proteins  that help move metabolites like pyruvate  from the inter membrane space into the  matrix of the mitochondria and the third  class are a highly specialized set of  enzyme complexes called  f1 f0 ATP a s that directly synthesizes  ATP given the relative difficulty of  moving molecules across the inner  mitochondrial membrane and into the  matrix specific transporter proteins are  required for moving small molecules and  ions across this membrane the  mitochondrion double membranes establish  two discrete and distinctive areas  within the organelle we saw that the  matrix is the volume enclosed by the  inner membrane making it the innermost  space within the mitochondrion while the  volume between the two membranes is  called the inter membrane  space the matrix contains hundreds of  enzymes involved in what's called the  citric acid cycle and it also contains  the mitochondrial DNA genome it is a far  more viscous environment compared to the  cytosol because it has a higher  concentration of soluble proteins it  plays a critical role in ATP synthesis  and in the process of so-called  oxidative phosphorylation the inter  membrane space has a similar consistency  as the cytosol  however the protein composition is very  different due to size selectivity of the  outer mitochondrial membrane there are  also proteins that are specifically  localized in the inter membrane space  cytochrome C is one such example of a  protein concentrated in this region  you  you  [Music]

